sections.s2_technical:
The Spring Boot startup sequence has 8 steps in order. First, `SpringApplication.run(MainClass.class, args)` is called from the `main` method. This creates a `SpringApplication` instance and triggers the startup process. Second, it creates the `ApplicationContext` — the IoC container. For web applications this is `AnnotationConfigServletWebServerApplicationContext`. Third, auto-configuration kicks in: Spring Boot scans the classpath, finds libraries (Tomcat, JPA, Redis), and registers matching `@Configuration` classes via `spring.factories` or `AutoConfiguration.imports`. Fourth, `@ComponentScan` discovers all `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Controller` classes and registers them as bean definitions. Fifth, singleton beans are instantiated in dependency order — constructor injection first, then `@PostConstruct` lifecycle callbacks.

```java
// Step 1: main method triggers everything
@SpringBootApplication
public class SettlementApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(SettlementApplication.class, args);
        // Creates ApplicationContext → auto-config → component scan → bean creation → server start
    }
}
```

Sixth, the embedded web server (Tomcat by default) is created and started. `DispatcherServlet` is registered as the front controller — every HTTP request goes through it. DispatcherServlet uses `HandlerMapping` to find the right `@Controller` method, calls `HandlerAdapter` to invoke it, and uses `ViewResolver` or `HttpMessageConverter` to produce the response.

```java
// DispatcherServlet flow:
// HTTP Request → DispatcherServlet
//   → HandlerMapping (find @RequestMapping match)
//   → HandlerAdapter (invoke controller method)
//   → HttpMessageConverter (serialize response to JSON)
//   → HTTP Response
```

Seventh, `CommandLineRunner` and `ApplicationRunner` beans are executed — one-time startup tasks like data loading or cache warming. Eighth, the `ApplicationReadyEvent` is published — the application is fully started and ready to serve requests.

Graceful shutdown (`server.shutdown=graceful` in application.yml) changes the shutdown behavior. When SIGTERM is received (K8s pod termination), the server stops accepting NEW connections but continues processing IN-FLIGHT requests for up to `spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase` (default 30s). In Kubernetes, a `preStop` hook adds a delay before SIGTERM so the pod is removed from the service endpoints first — preventing new requests from reaching a terminating pod.

```yaml
# Graceful shutdown configuration:
server:
  shutdown: graceful
spring:
  lifecycle:
    timeout-per-shutdown-phase: 30s
```

For deployment, Spring Boot produces a fat JAR (`mvn package` → `java -jar app.jar`) or a Docker image (`mvn spring-boot:build-image`). In K8s, readiness probe (`/actuator/health/readiness`) ensures traffic only reaches fully-started pods. Liveness probe (`/actuator/health/liveness`) restarts stuck pods.

