sections.s2_technical:
`BeanPostProcessor` intercepts **every** bean creation through two callbacks: `postProcessBeforeInitialization` (runs before `@PostConstruct`) and `postProcessAfterInitialization` (runs after). Spring uses it internally for `@Autowired` injection and for creating AOP proxies — this is exactly why `@Transactional` self-invocation fails: the proxy is wrapped in `postProcessAfterInitialization`, so calling a method on `this` bypasses it. You write a custom one to add logging or metrics to every bean.

```java
@Component
public class MetricsBeanPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String name) {
        // wrap/instrument every bean after it is fully initialized
        return bean;
    }
}
```

`ApplicationEventPublisher` + `@EventListener` decouples services **in-process**: a publisher fires an `ApplicationEvent`, and any `@EventListener` method in any bean receives it without the two knowing about each other.

```java
publisher.publishEvent(new SettlementCompleteEvent(count));

@EventListener
public void on(SettlementCompleteEvent e) { /* send email */ }
```

`SmartLifecycle` controls **startup/shutdown ordering** of background tasks via `getPhase()` (lower phase starts first, stops last) — e.g. start a Kafka consumer only after the DB is ready. Virtual threads in Spring Boot turn on with `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` (Java 21+): all request threads become virtual, scaling I/O-bound work cheaply but doing nothing for CPU-bound work. Spring Batch is **chunk-based**: `ItemReader` reads N items → `ItemProcessor` transforms each → `ItemWriter` writes the chunk in a single transaction; skip/retry handle faults.

